  SECURITY REPORT — Score: 68/100 [MEDIUM]
  The codebase contains several critical security issues, notably missing authentication on public endpoints, hard‑coded passwords, and an insecure direct object reference in user look‑ups. Additional high‑severity concerns include unrestricted CORS and lack of input length validation. Implementing proper auth checks, removing hard‑coded secrets, adding ownership validation, tightening CORS, enforcing field length limits, and adding rate limiting will markedly improve the security posture.

  Findings (7):
  [CRITICAL] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The /seed endpoint is publicly accessible and can be invoked by anyone without authentication, allowing an attacker to populate or overwrite demo data.
             Fix: Add `Depends(get_current_user)` (or an admin‑only dependency) to the endpoint and protect it with role‑based checks.
  [CRITICAL] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/stats_routes.py
             The statistics summary endpoint does not require authentication, exposing internal aggregate data to unauthenticated users.
             Fix: Add `current_user: User = Depends(get_current_user)` to the dependency list and enforce appropriate permissions.
  [CRITICAL] HardcodedSecret in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             Plain‑text passwords are hard‑coded for seeded users, which can be extracted from source code and used to compromise those accounts.
             Fix: Replace hard‑coded passwords with randomly generated secrets stored in environment variables or a secure secret manager; provide a one‑time password reset flow for seeded accounts.
  [CRITICAL] IDOR in app/routes/user_routes.py
             Any authenticated user can retrieve arbitrary user records by ID without ownership or role checks, leading to an insecure direct object reference.
             Fix: Validate that `user_id == current_user.id` or that the current user has an admin role before returning the user data.
  [HIGH    ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/user.py
             User‑provided strings (e.g., username, email, title, description) have no maximum length constraints, allowing excessively large payloads that can cause denial‑of‑service or buffer‑overflow style issues.
             Fix: Add `max_length` arguments (e.g., `Field(min_length=1, max_length=150)`) to all string fields in Pydantic schemas.
  [HIGH    ] CORSIssue in app/main.py
             The application allows requests from any origin, which is unsafe for production and can enable cross‑site request forgery attacks.
             Fix: Restrict `allow_origins` to a whitelist of trusted frontend domains or configure it via an environment variable.
  [MEDIUM  ] RateLimiting in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             Login and signup endpoints have no rate‑limiting, making them vulnerable to credential‑stuffing and brute‑force attacks.
             Fix: Integrate a rate‑limiting middleware (e.g., `slowapi` or `starlette-limiter`) and apply limits such as 5 attempts per minute per IP.

  Passed: No raw SQL string interpolation detected (SQLAlchemy ORM used safely), Passwords are hashed using a secure algorithm before storage
